id "q-340719" :question/problem "Complete the arguments in the reasoning below to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3}.$First, we apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=F(s)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nWe now examine the convergence of $F(s)$ for different values of $s:$\n\n$\\,$ For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-3}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-4}}\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $L{-\\frac{7}{3}}=-\\frac{7}{3s},$ \ndefined for\n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>0, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{{}_{0}}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{7}{3s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3s}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3}$ is $F(s)=-\\frac{7}{3s},$ defined for $s>0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-340719/field-1" "q-340719/field-2" "q-340719/field-3" "q-340719/field-4" "q-340719/field-5" "q-340719/field-6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4e3079d-80a6-5333-8304-7116b01de82c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-340719"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-340724/field-1/99ab5dfd0cf985cf17af80ffb77f3f53f7c7e564a7588acd51c847651206f566" :answer/id #uuid "5f00c38d-cdda-525a-83ba-1cdd0c465865" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(7-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340724/field-1/f4ba462fc6a8fe5567d48ec5c0e17f6992f2f5418b276ac0e5f82cc29601bd25" :answer/id #uuid "cad09560-ae7a-5d61-9001-fe8a26a91535" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{(7-s)t}\\,\\