40736/field-2/89cc92a5a256d32ea65399b5196bd1fc92b2d3f166799d13cfa09c67368e2d16" :answer/id #uuid "bb408b50-221c-50ff-b155-05f68d4c0fd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340736/field-2/42ced35e7ecb80008fbb907ca1c960b54b9f1c0d560dcec5a4066ee19f6f744e" :answer/id #uuid "235c7ff3-db7f-5028-8184-84cc717882aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s<0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340736/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "abe1e4ce-4154-576e-8834-9ffc882766f1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-340736/field-2/de63348529d477cd70740ab9581d04b231678372657a4ecc21dd12e21b5d033c" :answer-field/choices ["q-340736/field-2/de63348529d477cd70740ab9581d04b231678372657a4ecc21dd12e21b5d033c" "q-340736/field-2/23424e5dffaca1bffc81dd2cc015a958b04177158815d98fdd41149314b3d874" "q-340736/field-2/89cc92a5a256d32ea65399b5196bd1fc92b2d3f166799d13cfa09c67368e2d16" "q-340736/field-2/42ced35e7ecb80008fbb907ca1c960b54b9f1c0d560dcec5a4066ee19f6f744e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340736" :question/id #uuid "4efd2036-c489-52bf-89f9-36ddc55c16f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-340736" :question/problem "What is the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{4\\text{i}t},$ where $t,s\\in R?$$\\,L(f(t))=$\n{{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{4\\text{i}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{4\\text{i}t}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{4\\text{i}-s}{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\text{i}-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{4\\text{i}a}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{4\\text{i}t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-340736/field-1" "q-340736/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a90a0c43-d87f-5639-826d-854b90876a27"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-340736